  "why": "The statement asks that every even n be a gap infinitely often. Nothing credible settles that or any single n. I scored the bounded-gap results (Zhang, Maynard, Polymath, Stadlmann 240, Song–Yue 236, Axiom 212, OpenAI 186) as bound. Each one moves the least even n known to be a Polignac number, which is a quantity on this question, but none determines whether every even n is one. Pintz's density result is scored the same way, as a proportion. The claimed full proofs and claimed twin or cousin cases have several F10 signals each: math.GM or unreviewed venues, many patch revisions, scope changes, a self-contradicting 'unconditional' title, claims to beat the parity barrier, and no uptake while the field still treats n=2 as open. I set them aside as not_credible. Oehlschlaegel is withdrawn. For AI credit, the 186 event is generated: the only quote credits GPT-6 Astra with pushing the bound, and the tracker says the model produced the proof with human-guided refinement. The 212 event lists no AI system and its language describes formal verification and compute, so it is none. The Axiom 246 library is formalisation only.",
